---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Roofing from '@/components/tools/calc2/Roofing.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="roofing-calculator">
  <Roofing client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the roofing calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The roofing calculator takes the plan area of the roof (the footprint plus the overhang on every
      side) and multiplies it by the pitch factor. The pitch factor is the length of the slope for each
      unit of horizontal run. Every plane of a roof with one pitch has the same factor, so the same rule
      gives the area of a gable, hip or shed roof.
    </p>
    <pre><code>pitch factor = √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²)
plan area    = (length + 2 × overhang) × (width + 2 × overhang)
roof area    = plan area × pitch factor
squares      = roof area × (1 + waste ÷ 100) ÷ 100 ft²
bundles      = squares × 3, rounded up</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A house of 40 × 30 feet with a 6 in 12 gable roof and no overhang has a plan area of 1,200 ft². The
      pitch factor is √(1 + 0.5²) = 1.1180, so the roof area is 1,341.6 ft², or 13.42 squares. With 10 %
      waste that is 14.76 squares, which is 45 bundles of shingles.
    </p>

    <h2>Pitch factors</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Pitch</th><th>Angle</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>3 in 12</td><td>14.0°</td><td>1.031</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 in 12</td><td>18.4°</td><td>1.054</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6 in 12</td><td>26.6°</td><td>1.118</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8 in 12</td><td>33.7°</td><td>1.202</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10 in 12</td><td>39.8°</td><td>1.302</td></tr>
        <tr><td>12 in 12</td><td>45.0°</td><td>1.414</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The drawing</h2>
    <p>
      The house is drawn to scale in an isometric view, on walls of 8 feet (2.44 m), with shingle courses
      at a 5 inch exposure. When you change the pitch, the roof moves to the new pitch, and the triangle at
      the top left shows the rise over a run of 12.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>All planes have one pitch. For a roof with two pitches, calculate each part on its own and add them.</li>
      <li>Dormers, valleys and skylights are not modelled. Add waste for them, or measure them separately.</li>
      <li>Bundles assume three per square. Some heavy architectural shingles come four or five to a square; check the wrapper.</li>
      <li>Starter strips, ridge caps, drip edge and flashing are extra. The ridge and hip lengths help you order them.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
